course: dictionary
lesson: 5541
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Legendary is a good example. It can mean from an old story, very famous, or brilliant.
explain: «The legendary King Arthur» is from a legend, an old story that may or may not be true. «A legendary footballer» means very famous and admired. Read the words around the word to see which meaning fits.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «That party was legendary!» cannot mean from an old story. The speaker means it was amazing, so the word is praise.
fact: Legendary means from a legend, an old story that may or may not be true.
fact: Legendary can also mean very famous and admired, talked about by many people.
fact: In informal speech, legendary means brilliant or amazing, as in «That party was legendary!»
fact: The Yellow Emperor, Huangdi, is a legendary ruler said to have lived about 4,700 years ago. Historians cannot prove he lived.
fact: BC years count backwards. Rome's legendary founding in 753 BC came 709 years before Julius Caesar was killed in 44 BC.
exercise: «The legendary King Arthur ruled a great kingdom.» What does legendary mean here? | Brilliant at football | Very slow and tired | Made of wood and paint | *From an old story that may or may not be true
exercise: «In his game, Ollie opens loot boxes for a legendary skin.» What does legendary suggest about the skin? | *Extremely rare and highly prized | Old and cracked | Made from a legend | Very cheap and easy to find
exercise: Nadia says, «That party was legendary!» What does she mean? | *It was brilliant and amazing | It was very quiet and dull | It was cancelled at the last minute | It was held in a very old castle
test: «A legendary footballer» is praised by many fans. What does legendary mean here? | *Very famous and admired | Very cold | Lost in the crowd | From an old story
test: Which meaning fits «the legendary Yellow Emperor»? | Brilliant and amazing | Made of gold | Very fast | *From an old story that may or may not be true
exam: Which is the best way to check the meaning of a word with more than one meaning? | Count the letters in the word | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Guess the first meaning you think of and never check | Skip the word and never read that sentence again
